    <string name="pp_section_1_title">1. Data controller</string>
    <string name="pp_section_1_body">The controller of your personal data is %1$s. For matters relating to the processing of your data, please contact us at e-mail: %2$s.</string>
    <string name="pp_section_2_title">2. What data we collect</string>
    <string name="pp_section_2_body">"• Email address and account name (login) provided during registration.\n• First name, last name and avatar – only if you add them yourself in your profile.\n• Content you create in the app: added places, reviews, ratings, photos, reports and the approximate location of those places.\n• Google login data (if you choose to log in via Google) – we only receive your ID, email and avatar from Google.\n• Current location of your device – only when you consciously use the „Fetch my location” function when adding a place or viewing nearby places. We do not save location history.\n• Technical data: notification tokens, installation IDs, crash logs, diagnostic data, performance data and basic analytical events."</string>
    <string name="pp_section_3_title">3. For what purpose</string>
    <string name="pp_section_3_body">We use the data only for the operation of the app: logging in, displaying a map of kid-friendly places, presenting reviews from other parents, notifications, handling reports, fault diagnostics, performance improvement and the ranking of the most active users. We do not use your data for advertising or profiling.</string>
    <string name="pp_section_4_title">4. Who we share data with</string>
    <string name="pp_section_4_body">"• Google Firebase: Authentication, Firestore, Storage, Crashlytics, Analytics, Performance Monitoring, Cloud Messaging, App Check and Remote Config – as technical services needed for the account, database, photos, notifications, security, diagnostics and quality of the app.\n• Google Maps Platform and Google Play Services – for the map, location, Google login and system functions.\nGoogle servers may be located outside the European Economic Area; Google ensures an adequate level of protection within the mechanisms provided for by GDPR.\n• Other users of the app – see your login, avatar and content that you publish publicly (places, reviews). Your first and last name remain private."</string>
    <string name="pp_section_5_title">5. Your rights</string>
    <string name="pp_section_5_body">"You have the right to access your data, rectify it, delete it („the right to be forgotten”), restrict processing, portability and object. You can make most changes yourself from the Profile tab – including data editing and permanent account deletion along with reviews and places (the „Delete account” button).\n\nA public account deletion manual is available at https://playground-705e7162.web.app/account-deletion.\n\nIf for any reason you cannot perform these operations yourself – write to %1$s and we will help."</string>
    <string name="pp_section_6_title">6. Storage period</string>
    <string name="pp_section_6_body">Account data is stored until the account is deleted. Public content (places, reviews) may be retained in anonymized form if its removal would hinder the operation of the app for other users. Notification tokens are deleted or deactivated after logging out, account deletion or expiry. Diagnostic and performance data are stored only for the time needed for analysis of errors, security and quality.</string>
    <string name="pp_section_7_title">7. Security</string>
    <string name="pp_section_7_body">Communication of the app with servers takes place only through encrypted HTTPS connections. Your password is never sent or stored in plain text – Firebase Auth only keeps its hash. Avatars and photos go to a private Firebase Storage bucket, accessible only to logged-in users.</string>
    <string name="pp_section_8_title">8. Policy changes</string>
    <string name="pp_section_8_body">We will inform you of significant changes to the privacy policy in the app at the next launch after the implementation of changes, updating the „Effective from” date at the top of this document.</string>
